<h2>Test 5: The Three-Cone Drill (L Drill)</h2>
<div class="test-card">
<div class="test-card-header">
<div class="test-icon">🔺</div>
<div>
<div class="test-card-title">Three-Cone Drill (L Drill)</div>
<div class="test-card-sub">Change of direction &amp; agility</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="test-what">
<strong>What it measures:</strong> Agility, change of direction, and body control. Also called the L drill because of the path the athlete runs. Three cones are placed in an L-shape — one at the start, one 5 yards ahead, and one 5 yards to the right. The athlete runs to the first cone and back, then weaves around all three cones in a figure-eight pattern. It's the most technically demanding combine test and the one that most closely mimics football movement — sharp cuts, redirecting at full speed, and accelerating out of breaks.
      </div>
<div class="test-stats">
<div class="test-stat">
<div class="test-stat-val">~7.0s</div>
<div class="test-stat-label">Combine avg (skill positions)</div>
</div>
<div class="test-stat">
<div class="test-stat-val">Under 6.7s</div>
<div class="test-stat-label">Elite range</div>
</div>
<div class="test-stat">
<div class="test-stat-val">6.28s</div>
<div class="test-stat-label">All-time record (Chris Johnson, 2008)</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<h3>How the L Drill Works — Step by Step</h3>
<p>The three-cone drill route is specific and must be run correctly to be timed. Here's the exact path:</p>
<ol style="padding-left:1.25rem;margin-bottom:1.25rem">
<li style="font-size:0.97rem;color:var(--text-mid);margin-bottom:0.5rem"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Start</strong> at cone A in a three-point stance</li>
<li style="font-size:0.97rem;color:var(--text-mid);margin-bottom:0.5rem"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Sprint 5 yards</strong> to cone B, touch the ground</li>
<li style="font-size:0.97rem;color:var(--text-mid);margin-bottom:0.5rem"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Return 5 yards</strong> back to cone A, touch the ground</li>
<li style="font-size:0.97rem;color:var(--text-mid);margin-bottom:0.5rem"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Sprint 5 yards</strong> to cone B again</li>
<li style="font-size:0.97rem;color:var(--text-mid);margin-bottom:0.5rem"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Turn right</strong> and run around the outside of cone B</li>
<li style="font-size:0.97rem;color:var(--text-mid);margin-bottom:0.5rem"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Sprint 5 yards</strong> to cone C, weave around its outside</li>
<li style="font-size:0.97rem;color:var(--text-mid);margin-bottom:0.5rem"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Return 5 yards</strong> back to cone B, weave around its inside</li>
<li style="font-size:0.97rem;color:var(--text-mid);margin-bottom:0.5rem"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Finish</strong> by sprinting back 5 yards through the start line</li>
</ol>
<p>The weaving pattern around the cones is what makes this drill technically demanding — athletes who round corners or take poor angles lose significant time. Tight, controlled turns are the key to a fast L drill time.</p>
