<h3>Why Scouts Value the L Drill</h3>
<p>The L drill is highly predictive for <strong>cornerbacks, linebackers, and tight ends</strong> — positions that require rapid change of direction in space. A CB who runs a 6.45 L drill can mirror receivers through breaks far more effectively than one who runs a 6.85. For pass rushers, a fast L drill suggests the hip flexibility and quickness to turn the corner on pass rushing moves.</p>
<p>The drill is <strong>less predictive for offensive linemen and pure speed positions</strong> like wide receiver — a WR's route-running ability in games correlates more with catching ability and release technique than with L drill times.</p>
<p>One important note: the L drill requires significant technique to run correctly. Athletes who practice the pattern before the combine often run significantly faster times than those who run it cold. Unlike the 40-yard dash where technique matters less, the L drill rewards preparation specifically.</p>

<h2>Test 6: The 20-Yard Shuttle</h2>
<div class="test-card">
<div class="test-card-header">
<div class="test-icon">🔄</div>
